that are going to center on youth leadership and the patrol method.

27
00:02:39,920 --> 00:02:43,260
And the reason we're going to center on these, well, I'll talk about it again in a moment,

28
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:49,500
but this is like the most common question, the most common request that I get.

29
00:02:49,820 --> 00:02:54,980
So we're going to be talking about that in Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

30
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:56,380
Then I have an answer to an email.

31
00:02:56,780 --> 00:02:59,080
Who gets to decide whether you wear neckerchiefs or not?

32
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:04,760
Well, you're going to need to listen, and this will tell you exactly how that works.

33
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:06,840
Then we're going to wind up with a Scoutmasters minute.

34
00:03:07,260 --> 00:03:10,840
So there's a lot on the table, a lot to get done.

35
00:03:11,100 --> 00:03:12,540
I think we better get started, shall we?

36
00:03:12,540 --> 00:03:16,140
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

37
00:03:16,140 --> 00:03:29,420
I get a fair number of emails in response to the blog and in response to the podcast.

38
00:03:29,700 --> 00:03:32,380
I get comments on the blog and comments on the Facebook page.

39
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:37,320
And one of the most common questions that is the subject of these comments or emails

40
00:03:37,320 --> 00:03:43,859
is how to transition towards boy leadership in a troop that's being led by the adults

41
00:03:43,859 --> 00:03:50,400
or how to implement or, you know, people commonly say jumpstart the patrol system.

42
00:03:50,660 --> 00:03:52,540
Now, let me give you a little background here.

43
00:03:52,540 --> 00:03:57,640
I served on our camp staff for about 12 years, and two of those were as the camp director

44
00:03:57,640 --> 00:04:02,019
and most of the rest were in senior positions on the camp staff.

45
00:04:02,020 --> 00:04:07,540
And I say this because during that time, I got to see literally hundreds of troops operate

46
00:04:07,540 --> 00:04:08,060
for a week.

47
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:12,760
And I understand why these are such common questions, because most of the troops that I

48
00:04:12,760 --> 00:04:18,519
saw as a part of working on camp staff probably were not led by the youth, and most of them

49
00:04:18,519 --> 00:04:21,300
didn't really fully utilize the patrol method.

50
00:04:21,500 --> 00:04:23,000
So these are pretty common problems.

51
00:04:23,280 --> 00:04:28,400
And I want to take over the next several podcasts the opportunity to try and lay out as many answers

52
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:30,140
to these difficulties as possible.

53
00:04:30,140 --> 00:04:38,800
And I'm going to begin by saying what we have to do is realize why scouting is different

54
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:42,740
from all the other stuff that we do with our youth.

55
00:04:42,960 --> 00:04:44,640
It's different from sports teams.

56
00:04:44,840 --> 00:04:48,159
It's different from, you know, clubs and things like that.

57
00:04:48,219 --> 00:04:49,520
It's different from church groups.

58
00:04:49,880 --> 00:04:51,419
And how is it different?

59
00:04:51,700 --> 00:04:55,419
Well, I'm going to boil that down to three basic ways that scouting is different.

60
00:04:55,420 --> 00:05:00,740
The first way scouting is different is that scouts have all the leadership responsibility

61
00:05:00,740 --> 00:05:01,580
and authority.

62
00:05:02,060 --> 00:05:07,600
Adults are there to guide and to mentor, but not to lead in the purest sense of the word.

63
00:05:08,060 --> 00:05:11,120
Adults don't dole out responsibilities and authority to scouts.

64
00:05:11,640 --> 00:05:17,740
The program keeps authority and responsibility squarely on the shoulders of the youth leadership.

65
00:05:18,080 --> 00:05:21,460
Adults aren't there to present a program of activities to a bunch of boys.

66
00:05:21,460 --> 00:05:24,700
The scouts develop and present activities for themselves.

67
00:05:25,280 --> 00:05:31,260
The second way scouting is different is that it channels the instinctive way boys form groups.

68
00:05:31,620 --> 00:05:37,719
Boys form clubs and gangs and cliques and groups without prompting or direction from adults.

69
00:05:37,820 --> 00:05:39,060
They do this all the time.

70
00:05:39,419 --> 00:05:41,599
They have done this from time immemorial.

71
00:05:41,979 --> 00:05:47,700
And our founder, Baden Powell, recognized this and decided it could be a really positive thing.

72
00:05:47,700 --> 00:05:51,260
But the rest of the world doesn't really like it very much.

73
00:05:51,640 --> 00:05:56,140
You know, schooling, for the most part, tries to break up those natural groups because boys

74
00:05:56,140 --> 00:05:57,300
can use them for good and evil.

75
00:05:57,560 --> 00:06:02,039
And to tell you what, most of the time, they use them for not quite so good stuff.

76
00:06:02,360 --> 00:06:06,800
The third way scouting is different is that while it has goals and method, it relies on

77
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:13,080
the natural curiosity of boys, their natural desire to do good and to be good and to make

78
00:06:13,080 --> 00:06:14,640
good and to be accepted.

79
00:06:14,640 --> 00:06:19,479
Boys discover these things best when they have the autonomy to do so rather than when

80
00:06:19,479 --> 00:06:21,340
they are compelled to do so.

81
00:06:21,500 --> 00:06:23,979
How do these three differences affect what we do?

82
00:06:24,200 --> 00:06:28,919
Well, these differences are somewhat antithetical to the rest of the way the world works.

83
00:06:29,140 --> 00:06:34,320
Corporations and schools and teams and clubs and the like have some pretty structured hierarchical

84
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:40,180
leadership that comes from the adults who organize the team or the corporation or the school

85
00:06:40,180 --> 00:06:40,680
or whatever.

86
00:06:40,680 --> 00:06:45,060
They have highly focused goals and tightly defined practices.

87
00:06:45,520 --> 00:06:48,840
All of this is really our default setting as human beings.

88
00:06:49,140 --> 00:06:50,680
And it's the default setting of boys.

89
00:06:50,980 --> 00:06:55,540
You put a group of boys in a room with an adult and they will default to the leadership of the

90
00:06:55,540 --> 00:06:55,800
adult.

91
00:06:55,900 --> 00:07:00,580
They'll sit and listen and be marginally cooperative because that's what they have been conditioned

92
00:07:00,580 --> 00:07:01,100
to do.

93
00:07:01,640 --> 00:07:03,580
I mean, they've been conditioned to do that in school.

94
00:07:03,700 --> 00:07:05,460
They've been conditioned to do that in home.

95
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:06,580
And it's not totally negative.

96
00:07:06,580 --> 00:07:12,380
I'm not saying that this is a bad thing necessarily, but we have to realize that our mere presence

97
00:07:12,380 --> 00:07:15,419
has an effect on what boys do.

98
00:07:15,580 --> 00:07:20,659
It takes some real effort and time to overturn this default setting and develop the kind of

99
00:07:20,659 --> 00:07:23,440
mutual trust that's needed to make scouting work.

100
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:30,060
So the first step in developing a scout-led troop with a strong patrol system is to educate

101
00:07:30,060 --> 00:07:30,680
adults.

102
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,979
Not scouts, but we need to educate adults.

103
00:07:33,980 --> 00:07:39,280
Now, I see this happen each year as new adults join along with their sons.

104
00:07:39,640 --> 00:07:43,260
Most of the time, they're coming from a position of leadership in Cub Scouts and they're used

105
00:07:43,260 --> 00:07:46,180
to doing the leading and setting the program and making the decision.

106
00:07:46,840 --> 00:07:51,760
It usually takes some time and experience for them to understand how patrols work and how

107
00:07:51,760 --> 00:07:52,780
a scout troop is led.

108
00:07:52,980 --> 00:07:56,660
I inevitably have to talk them through this as they often find it kind of difficult.

109
00:07:57,140 --> 00:07:59,580
But in a year or so, they've got the idea.

110
00:07:59,580 --> 00:08:04,219
The first step then in educating adults is getting them to trust the scouting program.

111
00:08:04,599 --> 00:08:08,419
They can trust it without even understanding it because it takes some time to learn to

112
00:08:08,419 --> 00:08:09,200
understand it.

113
00:08:09,460 --> 00:08:13,659
When boys lead a troop or patrol, they do it imperfectly and haltingly and in small steps

114
00:08:13,659 --> 00:08:16,219
and it doesn't look right, mistakes are made, but they do it.

115
00:08:16,460 --> 00:08:21,520
Adults have to watch this evolution once or twice before they can really accept that it

116
00:08:21,520 --> 00:08:21,880
works.

117
00:08:22,120 --> 00:08:26,200
So let's say you're a scoutmaster who's been at it for a few years or a new scoutmaster and

118
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:31,580
you want to make a commitment to having a real, fully functional, scout-led patrol system

119
00:08:31,580 --> 00:08:32,059
troop.

120
00:08:32,260 --> 00:08:36,100
Well, you need to understand and trust the program and then let it go to work.

121
00:08:36,419 --> 00:08:41,100
And to understand the program, you have to approach it free of preconceived notions and

122
00:08:41,100 --> 00:08:42,439
just look at it for what it is.

123
00:08:42,659 --> 00:08:46,300
This is especially difficult if you've been a scoutmaster for a length of time and you're

124
00:08:46,300 --> 00:08:47,819
used to doing things a certain way.

125
00:08:47,819 --> 00:08:52,939
You have to lay all of your experience and your preconceptions aside and look at the program

126
00:08:52,939 --> 00:08:54,200
with fresh eyes.

127
00:08:54,200 --> 00:08:59,620
That's where we're going to begin next time around in talking about how to have strong

128
00:08:59,620 --> 00:09:03,640
youth leadership and how to implement the patrol system within your troop.

129
00:09:03,640 --> 00:09:15,560
Boy Scouts!

130
00:09:15,560 --> 00:09:19,520
Boy Scouts!

131
00:09:19,520 --> 00:09:26,900
Boy Scouts!

132
00:09:26,900 --> 00:09:31,240
Write me a letter, send it by name.

133
00:09:32,819 --> 00:09:34,380
Email, that is, folks.

134
00:09:34,980 --> 00:09:38,040
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

135
00:09:38,040 --> 00:09:46,079
So here's a recent email I received.

136
00:09:47,260 --> 00:09:49,980
And this is from an anonymous scouter out there.

137
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:55,640
They say, my scouts saw on the BSA uniform grade sheet,

138
00:09:55,819 --> 00:09:58,680
that would be the uniform inspection form, I think,

139
00:09:58,719 --> 00:10:01,140
if we were looking for the exact proper term,

140
00:10:01,760 --> 00:10:04,920
that the wearing of neckerchiefs was a troop decision.

141
00:10:04,920 --> 00:10:09,199
Now, I understand this to mean that this is the scouts call.

142
00:10:09,479 --> 00:10:12,479
And I told them so, trying to support the boy-led troop thing.

143
00:10:12,800 --> 00:10:17,199
Well, my committee chair says that the boys must submit the recommendation to the committee

144
00:10:17,199 --> 00:10:21,040
for consideration of a change to the troop bylaws.

145
00:10:21,500 --> 00:10:26,819
I tried to explain that this was not something for the committee to consider or approve or vote on,

146
00:10:27,079 --> 00:10:30,420
but rather to acknowledge and incorporate into the bylaws.

147
00:10:30,780 --> 00:10:32,839
And the discussion of bylaws is another issue.

148
00:10:32,840 --> 00:10:35,300
But anyway, what are your thoughts?

149
00:10:35,700 --> 00:10:36,220
Okay.

150
00:10:36,800 --> 00:10:38,320
So this is what I replied.

151
00:10:38,780 --> 00:10:40,480
And let me digress for a moment.

152
00:10:40,880 --> 00:10:41,280
Surprise.

153
00:10:41,560 --> 00:10:42,620
I'm going to digress.

154
00:10:42,940 --> 00:10:47,440
But I've been down the bylaws road before, and I don't think they're a good idea.

155
00:10:47,800 --> 00:10:49,700
I know that's a big surprise to everybody.

156
00:10:49,920 --> 00:10:55,000
But if bylaws and other big, giant organizational documents and things like that

157
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:58,180
were useful to advancing the spirit and the goals of scouting,

158
00:10:58,180 --> 00:11:03,260
we would hear about them in training, and they would be part of our literature.

159
00:11:03,599 --> 00:11:06,719
There would be examples of good bylaws to have and things like that.

160
00:11:07,040 --> 00:11:11,000
To my knowledge, we don't hear about them in training, and they're not in the literature.

161
00:11:11,339 --> 00:11:12,780
And to me, there's enough said.

162
00:11:12,979 --> 00:11:18,079
It's not something that we need, and it's something that tends to cause more problems than it solves.

163
00:11:18,080 --> 00:11:24,400
Here's chapter and verse on the neckerchief issue, and this is from the BSA Uniform and Insignia Guide.

164
00:11:24,660 --> 00:11:29,400
And this is easy to find at www.scouting.org.

165
00:11:29,620 --> 00:11:34,640
Look for the BSA Uniform and Insignia Guide, and you'll find that this is what it says.

166
00:11:34,760 --> 00:11:36,460
Boy Scout neckerchiefs are optional.

167
00:11:36,760 --> 00:11:40,540
The troop decides by vote, and all the members abide by the decision.

168
00:11:40,820 --> 00:11:42,860
That's from the guide, verbatim.

169
00:11:43,020 --> 00:11:46,520
Here's another statement that you'll find in the Uniform and Insignia Guide.

170
00:11:46,520 --> 00:11:53,020
It says, the following pages contain our uniform policy as taken from the rules and regulations of the Boy Scouts of America.

171
00:11:53,720 --> 00:12:02,340
Neither of the rules and regulations of the Boy Scouts of America, the policy, nor the program applications may be added to or changed in any way,

172
00:12:02,560 --> 00:12:08,480
in any way, unless approved by the National Executive Board of the Boy Scouts of America through its Program Group Committee.

173
00:12:08,720 --> 00:12:14,460
These two statements obviously are not modified by troop bylaws or procedures or traditions.

174
00:12:14,460 --> 00:12:21,180
As your Scouts have discovered, they get to choose whether or not they want to wear neckerchiefs by voting on the question.

175
00:12:21,520 --> 00:12:25,620
And if the committee chair in this case wants to follow the rules, there they are.

176
00:12:25,880 --> 00:12:32,440
Things kind of go off the rails in Scouting when adults misunderstand the nature of authority in Scouting.

177
00:12:32,660 --> 00:12:40,340
And adults are only present in Scouting to offer their assistance to the Scouts in leading themselves, not to be their leaders.

178
00:12:40,340 --> 00:12:47,080
Now, I forgot exactly where I got this from, but if you look at the position badges in Scouting, which badges say leader?

179
00:12:47,360 --> 00:12:50,720
Not the Scoutmaster badge, not the Assistant Scoutmaster, not the committee.

180
00:12:51,320 --> 00:12:51,520
No.

181
00:12:51,700 --> 00:12:57,520
All the badges of office that are given to Scouts are the ones that say leader on them.

182
00:12:57,960 --> 00:13:01,960
Senior Patrol Leader, Assistant Senior Patrol Leader, Patrol Leader.

183
00:13:01,960 --> 00:13:03,060
So that should be a hint.

184
00:13:03,260 --> 00:13:10,140
The authority in Scouting is not something that is given totally to the adults to dole out to Scouts at their discretion.

185
00:13:10,540 --> 00:13:16,480
The program puts the authority in Scouting right in the hands of your youth leaders.

186
00:13:16,840 --> 00:13:17,980
And that's where we need to keep it.

187
00:13:18,180 --> 00:13:21,080
So there's your answer, and I hope that was helpful.

188
00:13:21,080 --> 00:13:28,900
Have you ever wandered lonely through the woods?

189
00:13:30,480 --> 00:13:33,720
And everything that feels just as it should

190
00:13:33,720 --> 00:13:39,800
You've bought of a life that you've bought of something good

191
00:13:39,800 --> 00:13:44,520
If you've ever wandered lonely through the woods

192
00:13:44,520 --> 00:13:55,180
If you've ever wandered lonely through the woods

193
00:13:55,180 --> 00:14:03,920
That's right, it's time for a Scoutmaster's Minute

194
00:14:03,920 --> 00:14:07,079
I'm on my hands

195
00:14:07,080 --> 00:14:19,760
As we assembled for our Vespers observance one evening on a campout around our campfire

196
00:14:19,760 --> 00:14:22,940
I asked everyone to bring a stick with them

197
00:14:22,940 --> 00:14:24,160
Just a small stick

198
00:14:24,160 --> 00:14:27,020
Once they were seated, we gathered all the sticks into the bundle

199
00:14:27,020 --> 00:14:29,180
And I held them as I stood in front of the fire

200
00:14:29,180 --> 00:14:31,480
None of the sticks tried to jump out of the bundle

201
00:14:31,480 --> 00:14:33,800
None of the other sticks tried to shove the others out of the way

202
00:14:33,800 --> 00:14:36,120
They just made a bundle without complaining

203
00:14:36,120 --> 00:14:38,280
And I said this to the scouts

204
00:14:38,280 --> 00:14:39,820
And I said, when there's a job to be done

205
00:14:39,820 --> 00:14:41,480
We need to get at it without complaining

206
00:14:41,480 --> 00:14:43,340
We're together as a troop or a patrol

207
00:14:43,340 --> 00:14:44,580
Everybody is important

208
00:14:44,580 --> 00:14:46,320
No one is excluded

209
00:14:46,320 --> 00:14:50,420
I asked the senior patrol leader to pick a stick out of the bundle

210
00:14:50,420 --> 00:14:51,940
And see if he could break it into two

211
00:14:51,940 --> 00:14:53,600
And he managed to do this pretty easily

212
00:14:53,600 --> 00:14:55,760
Then I handed him the whole bundle

213
00:14:55,760 --> 00:14:57,920
And I asked him to break all the sticks at once

214
00:14:57,920 --> 00:14:59,100
And try as he could

215
00:14:59,100 --> 00:15:00,300
It was impossible

216
00:15:00,300 --> 00:15:02,160
A single stick's easy to break

217
00:15:02,160 --> 00:15:04,260
But a bundle of sticks can't be broken

218
00:15:04,260 --> 00:15:06,060
We're strong when we keep together

219
00:15:06,060 --> 00:15:08,079
We can stand up to greater challenges

220
00:15:08,079 --> 00:15:10,760
And bear more difficulties as a troop or a patrol

221
00:15:10,760 --> 00:15:12,300
Than as individuals

222
00:15:12,300 --> 00:15:14,439
The senior patrol leader then chose a stick

223
00:15:14,439 --> 00:15:15,660
And I asked him to bend it

224
00:15:15,660 --> 00:15:16,920
Some sticks bend

225
00:15:16,920 --> 00:15:18,260
Some sticks break

226
00:15:18,260 --> 00:15:21,239
Learning to adapt our skills in changing circumstances

227
00:15:21,239 --> 00:15:23,439
And tempering our strength with flexibility

228
00:15:23,439 --> 00:15:26,339
Makes us valuable members of our troop and patrol

229
00:15:26,339 --> 00:15:28,000
And then I took the bundle of sticks

230
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:29,520
And laid them on the fire

231
00:15:29,520 --> 00:15:32,060
Warmth, support, and encouragement

232
00:15:32,060 --> 00:15:33,400
Come from working together

233
00:15:33,400 --> 00:15:37,739
Just as these sticks bring light and heat and cheer to the fire

234
00:15:37,739 --> 00:15:39,560
Once the sticks are burning

235
00:15:39,560 --> 00:15:40,880
I recovered one

236
00:15:40,880 --> 00:15:42,660
And laid it apart on the ground

237
00:15:42,660 --> 00:15:43,619
On our own

238
00:15:43,619 --> 00:15:45,619
The spark of intensity dims

239
00:15:45,619 --> 00:15:48,260
I said as the stick started to go out

240
00:15:48,260 --> 00:15:51,079
We need the support and encouragement of our troop and patrol

241
00:15:51,079 --> 00:15:52,719
To maintain our enthusiasm

242
00:15:52,720 --> 00:15:55,340
So let's spell the word sticks

243
00:15:55,340 --> 00:15:58,280
And think about what each letter can represent

244
00:15:58,280 --> 00:16:00,900
S means supporting each other

245
00:16:00,900 --> 00:16:02,980
Sustains our spirit

246
00:16:02,980 --> 00:16:06,100
T means together we can take on any task

247
00:16:06,100 --> 00:16:10,900
I means intensity increases when everyone is included

248
00:16:10,900 --> 00:16:15,140
C means combined courage combats challenges

249
00:16:15,140 --> 00:16:19,579
And K means keep flexible in changing circumstances

250
00:16:19,579 --> 00:16:22,319
And S is for stay at it

251
00:16:22,319 --> 00:16:23,340
And always remember

252
00:16:23,340 --> 00:16:25,260
To stick together

253
00:16:40,900 --> 00:17:03,280
Well thanks so much for listening to this edition of the Scoutmaster Podcast

254
00:17:03,280 --> 00:17:05,119
Now here's how to keep in touch

255
00:17:05,119 --> 00:17:07,819
Read the blog at scoutmastercg.com

256
00:17:07,819 --> 00:17:11,480
And while you're there you can subscribe to receive updates to the blog by email

257
00:17:11,480 --> 00:17:14,899
You can also follow the wonders of scoutmastercg.com

258
00:17:14,899 --> 00:17:17,399
In the vast panorama of social media

259
00:17:17,399 --> 00:17:21,919
You can join the nearly 13,000 folks who follow us on Facebook

260
00:17:21,919 --> 00:17:26,520
The 1,500 or so folks who tweet together with us on Twitter

261
00:17:26,520 --> 00:17:29,419
The 2,500 prolific pinners

262
00:17:29,419 --> 00:17:33,120
Who along with us pin pultitudinous pictures on Pinterest

263
00:17:33,120 --> 00:17:35,740
You can watch us at ScoutmasterCG on YouTube

264
00:17:35,740 --> 00:17:38,160
But listen, if you can't face Facebook

265
00:17:38,160 --> 00:17:39,300
And Twitter makes you bitter

266
00:17:39,300 --> 00:17:40,740
And if you've no interest in Pinterest

267
00:17:40,740 --> 00:17:43,040
If you don't hit the U in YouTube

268
00:17:43,040 --> 00:17:44,560
Well that's okay, Bunky

269
00:17:44,560 --> 00:17:47,020
Just zoom into the late 90s

270
00:17:47,020 --> 00:17:49,060
And fire off a good old-fashioned email

271
00:17:49,060 --> 00:17:53,140
I'm available at Clark at scoutmastercg.com

272
00:17:53,140 --> 00:17:56,400
C-L-A-R-K-E

273
00:17:56,400 --> 00:18:01,660
At scoutmastercg.com

274
00:18:01,660 --> 00:18:03,040
Now if all else fails

275
00:18:03,040 --> 00:18:05,200
You can write your message on a piece of paper

276
00:18:05,200 --> 00:18:06,580
Seal it in a bottle

277
00:18:06,580 --> 00:18:08,460
And bury it in any campfire circle

278
00:18:08,460 --> 00:18:09,700
I'll be sure to get it

279
00:18:09,700 --> 00:18:11,180
I have agents everywhere

280
00:18:11,180 --> 00:18:11,980
I assure you

281
00:18:11,980 --> 00:18:13,580
Now by this time

282
00:18:13,580 --> 00:18:14,940
It must be abundantly clear

283
00:18:14,940 --> 00:18:16,400
That the Scoutmaster blog

284
00:18:16,400 --> 00:18:17,560
And the Scoutmaster podcast

285
00:18:17,560 --> 00:18:20,260
Are not official publications

286
00:18:20,260 --> 00:18:21,660
Of any scouting organization

287
00:18:21,660 --> 00:18:22,560
Nor are they endorsed

288
00:18:22,560 --> 00:18:24,400
Or sponsored by any scouting organization

289
00:18:24,400 --> 00:18:27,120
No, it's just me with a microphone

290
00:18:27,120 --> 00:18:29,040
Trying to lend a hand to scout leaders

291
00:18:29,040 --> 00:18:31,120
And have a little bit of fun along the way

292
00:18:31,120 --> 00:18:31,879
And speaking of fun

293
00:18:31,879 --> 00:18:33,120
Here he is, our founder

294
00:18:33,120 --> 00:18:35,280
Sir Robert Baden-Powell

295
00:18:35,280 --> 00:18:36,320
Sir Robert

296
00:18:36,320 --> 00:18:37,760
Good luck to you

297
00:18:37,760 --> 00:18:39,040
And good camping

298
00:18:39,040 --> 00:18:40,620
Why, thank you, sir

299
00:18:40,620 --> 00:18:42,820
Until next time, everyone

300
00:18:42,820 --> 00:19:02,200
Thanks and friends

301
00:19:03,760 --> 00:19:09,679
Go

302
00:19:09,679 --> 00:19:09,879
Go

